  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nWe face the challenge of minimizing the overlap integral C5 for a step function h defined on [0,2] with the constraint that \u222bh = 1. The current record is 0.38085, and we aim to reach 0.380876.\n\nThe problem involves finding a step function h that minimizes the maximum overlap with its shifted versions (1-h(x+k)). The current implementation uses random initializations and local optimization (SLSQP). However, this approach may get stuck in local optima and hasn't achieved the desired 0.380876 in the given budget.\n\nThe proposed approach uses a multi-start optimization combined with polynomial interpolation and adaptive component blending. This strategy:\n1. Starts from the parent solution (if available)\n2. Explores new regions by interpolation and perturbation\n3. Maintains multiple promising candidates\n4. Adapts to promising areas by re-weighting optimization efforts\n\nThe key advantages are:\n- Polynomial interpolation reduces the need for gradient calculation\n- Adaptive weighting ensures focus on improving regions\n- Multiple candidates prevent premature convergence\n- Budget is efficiently used by prioritizing promising candidates\n\nThis should navigate towards the target C5 < 0.380876 while staying computationally feasible within the 100-second budget.\n\nMathematical specification\nWe represent h as a vector of n_points values (n_points=280). Each candidate h satisfies:\n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n- \u2211h[i] = n_points/2 (TARGET_SUM = 140)\n\nObjective function for a candidate h:\nC5 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\nComputation via polynomial cross-correlation scaled by dx=2.0/280\n\nOptimization uses:\n- Interpolation-based exploration (with one-dimensional interpolation in promising residue directions)\n- Adaptive candidate management (weighting based on improvement rate)\n- Repair mechanism maintaining integral constraint and bounds\n\nImplementation procedure\n1. Initial Setup:\n   - Import necessary libraries\n   - Set n_points=280, TARGET_SUM=140.0, budget=100s\n   - Validate and incorporate parent solution if available\n\n2. Initialization (using parent solution weighting coefficient alpha=0.5):\n   - Create initial population:\n        * n_candidates=5 (one parent, four perturbed)\n   - Compute fitness (C5) for each candidate\n\n3. Core optimization loop (until time or no improvement):\n   a. Evaluate fitness for all candidates\n   b. Select top candidates (by fitness)\n   c. Repair candidates to ensure constraints if needed:\n        while integral not close enough:\n            scale(candidate) to ANDROID 140\n        validate bounds by clipping and adjusting\n   d. Update candidate weights based on fitness:\n        decay weight for stale improvements\n        increase weight for better fitness\n   e. Add new interpolation candidates:\n        for each pair of candidates:\n            create interpolated candidate\n            compute fitness\n            incorporate if sufficiently better\n   f. Add new perturbation candidates:\n        globally perturb worst candidates\n        repair and evaluate\n   g. Update best solution if found\n\n4. Repair Function:\n   - Clip values to [0,1]\n   - Scale to TARGET_SUM, repeated until stable\n   - Handle edge cases (sum=0)\n\n5. Termination:\n   - Break if time exceeded\n   - Return best found candidate and its fitness\n\nSearch choices and useful variations\n- Default parameters:\n   n_candidates=5\n   n_int=3 (interpolations per good candidate)\n   n_pert=3 (perturbations per bad candidate)\n   weight_decay=0.95 (staleness decay)\n   improvement_threshold=0.05 (min improvement needed)\n\n- Fitness calculation uses:\n   correlation = np.correlate(np.clip(h,0,1), 1-np.clip(h,0,1), mode='full')\n   C5 = np.max(correlation) * (2.0/n_points)\n   np.isfinite(C5) is required\n\nComputebudget and robustness\n- Core operations: correlation (O(n log n), repair (O(n))\n- Estimates: ~20-30 seconds for core optimization (strong candidate variances)\n- Fallback: parent or better extant solution\n- Validation: always recompute C5 for final candidate\n- Safety: enforce repair, validate constraints\n\nValidation and return contract\nEnsure feasibility:\n   np.isfinite(C5)\n   np.all(0 <= h[i] <= 1 for all i)\n   np.close(np.sum(h), 140)\nEvaluate: repeat C5 calculation on final candidate\nReturn format: (h_values, C5_bound, n_points) with all constraints satisfied.\n\nAll implementation-specific details contained in procedure section.",
